        "id": 1260470
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-07-18T23:50:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hm, actually I do have one idea. %p is the allowable way to extend printf. So you could use %pT to do all the same timestamping tricks we do today. The only difference is you need to pass a void pointer as an argument. So we could make the semantics this: pass NULL and printf will go get the current timestamp for you, or pass a pointer to a 64-bit timestamp you want to print.\n\nThere will still be changes though, since I need to convert every instance of %T to %pT, and pass a NULL along with it. But maybe that\u0027s better than the function call?",
      "parentUuid": "9c556508_34a5755c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 118,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "0ab02441dc7eb1263fed95935d63bce7ad92e35a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
